  "description": "Tie at the finalist boundary must never be broken by insertion order: the newer suggestion wins on approval over the older one",
  "spec_clause": "Phase 1 / Selecting finalists: 'Ties at the finalist boundary (e.g. 5th and 6th place with equal yes-votes) are common in a small group and must be broken deterministically, never by insertion order. Reuse the runoff's chain: higher approval, then rotation fairness, then shortest runtime, then seeded random.'",
  "rationale": "N_FINALISTS = 2, 5 attendees. m1 (added 2026-06-01): 5 rows, 4 yes -> approval 0.80, yes 4 -> slot 1. m3 (added 2026-06-02, i.e. the OLDER of the tied pair): 5 rows, 3 yes -> approval 3/5 = 0.60. m2 (added 2026-07-20, the NEWEST movie in the pool): 4 rows, 3 yes -> coverage 4/5 = 0.80, approval 3/4 = 0.75. m2 and m3 tie on yes-votes 3 for the last slot. Insertion order (or added_at, or id order) would admit m3; the spec forbids that. Rung 1 approval: m2 0.75 > m3 0.60 -> m2 admitted, m3 excluded. Runoff over {m1,m2}: m1 preferred by u1,u2,u5 = 3, m2 by u3,u4 = 2 -> Condorcet winner m1.",
